> Could you clarify whether the small-degree cases will be the necessary part of the proof for the generalization, or it will be superseded and replaced?

@wwylele , the method used in this pr will not generalize to the general higher order (order > 2) cases. This pr is intended to produce a reduced version and hopefully moving one small step towards the right direction. It can be viewed as the special case. In many applications (e.g. control theory), systems are 1st or 2nd order; and this pr covers a good portion of practical applications. The generalized proof would requires Cauchy index etc. which are not in math lib yet - as noted in the pr description.

The small-degree proofs will likely be superseded as proof steps once the general theorem is formalized, but could remain as corollaries or special cases of it.
